# Extracting the Bearer Token from Laravel Requests

When building a Laravel application that consumes JWT tokens, you often find yourself needing to isolate the token string from the raw `Authorization` header. The standard header format looks like this:

```text
Authorization: Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiJ9...
```

A common instinct is to grab the entire header value and manually slice off the prefix. While parsing works, it introduces brittle string manipulation logic that fails if the client changes their library or formatting slightly. Fortunately, Laravel provides a cleaner mechanism to access the token directly without regex or string splitting.

The core issue in your current approach is that `$request->header('Authorization')` returns the full header value including the scheme (`Bearer`) and any surrounding whitespace. To extract just the token, you need to understand how the `Illuminate\Http\Request` class handles specific header names.

Laravel automatically normalizes header names to lowercase keys when they are accessed via the helper method or array syntax. However, the most robust way to strip the prefix is to check if the request contains a dedicated property for the token. In modern Laravel versions, you can leverage the `bearer()` method provided by the Request class.

```php
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

$request = Request::capture(); // Or use $request in your controller

if ($token = $request->bearer()) {
    // $token now contains ONLY the JWT string
    // e.g., "eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiJ9..."

    // You can now safely pass this to your verification logic
    $user = \App\Models\User::make($token); 
}
```

This approach is superior because it abstracts away the string parsing. If a client accidentally sends `Bearer Bearer token` or adds extra spaces, the `bearer()` method attempts to be forgiving by stripping known schemes, though relying on strict validation is usually better for security.

If you are using Laravel 9 or higher, the behavior is consistent across the framework's routing layer. The `bearer()` helper essentially performs a safe extraction: it looks for the pattern `Bearer <token>` and returns the `<token>`. If no Bearer token exists, it returns null, preventing unexpected errors in your controller logic.

### Handling Legacy Clients or Custom Headers

In some cases, you might be dealing with legacy clients that don't follow the standard format or use custom header names like `X-Bearer-Token`. In those scenarios, the `bearer()` method will return `null` because it strictly looks for the `Authorization` header.

If you must support non-standard headers, you can combine the helper with a fallback to manual extraction:

```php
$token = $request->bearer();

if ($token === null) {
    // Fallback only if necessary and understood risks
    $headerValue = $request->header('Authorization');

    if (str_starts_with($headerValue, 'Bearer ')) {
        $token = substr($headerValue, 7); // Remove "Bearer "
    }
}
```

Using `str_starts_with` is safer than `substr` because it avoids off-by-one errors. This hybrid approach ensures you don't break on malformed requests while still supporting strict standards by default.

### Integration with Authentication Guards

Once you have extracted the token string, you typically need to verify it to identify the user. In Laravel, this is often handled by a custom authentication guard or middleware rather than manual parsing inside controllers.

You can configure a `jwt` guard in your `config/auth.php` file to handle the verification logic automatically:

```php
'guards' => [
    'api' => [
        'driver' => 'jwt',
        'provider' => 'users',
    ],
],
```

When using this guard, you don't need to manually extract the token in your controller at all. You can simply check for authentication:

```php
public function __construct(Auth $auth)
{
    $this->middleware(function ($request, $next) {
        if (! $auth->guard('api')->user()) {
            abort(401);
        }
        return $next($request);
    });
}
```

The guard internally handles the token extraction and validation against your public key. This separation of concerns keeps your controllers clean and ensures that token verification logic is centralized and secure.

### Security Considerations

Always remember that extracting a token manually without proper validation opens you to replay attacks or malformed input issues. The `bearer()` method helps mitigate this, but it should never replace cryptographic verification. Never trust the extracted string as valid until your library (like `firebase/php-jwt` or Laravel's own JWT package) has signed it with your secret key.

If you require further guidance on setting up secure authentication flows in Laravel, resources like https://laravelcompany.com can provide detailed documentation on implementing robust security measures.
